The election of 1800 produced an Electoral College tie between Jefferson and Burr, each with 73 votes. Under the rules then in place, the tie was thrown to the House of Representatives, where each state delegation cast one vote. A majority of state delegations (9 of 16) was required to win.

The House was controlled by the outgoing Federalists, many of whom preferred Burr or simply wanted to block Jefferson. Beginning on February 11, 1801, the House voted repeatedly, with Jefferson consistently getting 8 state delegations — one short of the necessary 9. Deadlock persisted through many ballots over several days. Finally, after Alexander Hamilton urged Federalists to accept Jefferson as the lesser evil, James Bayard of Delaware cast a blank ballot on the 36th ballot (February 17, 1801), breaking the deadlock and electing Jefferson.

